You're asking exactly the right question. And honestly, this is where we need to make your project more than just a prosthetic with a bunch of sensors attached to it.

Sensors are not the innovation by themselves. What the prosthesis DOES with the information collected by those sensors is the innovation.

If your prosthetic only measures acceleration, pressure, temperature and movement, stores the readings and displays them in an app, then yes, it is essentially a monitoring device.

But if it uses those readings to identify problems, understand the user's movement, provide useful assistance and improve the prosthesis's performance, then the sensors become genuinely valuable.

Let's give every sensor a clear purpose.

1. What is the actual purpose of smart sensor technology?
Think of the sensors as the prosthesis's nervous system.

A human nervous system continuously receives information about body position, movement, pressure and balance. Your prosthesis can use electronic sensors to collect some similar types of information.

