Here are four actual applications I would develop:

1. Personalised gait intelligence
The system learns the user's normal walking pattern.

For example:

The user's normal walking cadence is established.

The system records stride timing and movement.

It recognises when walking speed changes.

It identifies deviations from the user's baseline.

It provides information to the adaptive controller.

This can eventually help personalise mechanical assistance rather than relying on identical settings for every user.

2. Intelligent pressure management
Combine socket pressure, temperature and humidity readings.

For example:

A user has been wearing the prosthesis for several hours. The system identifies an increasing pressure concentration and elevated humidity.

Instead of simply displaying numbers, the application provides a message:

Socket condition alert

Unusual pressure concentration detected around one monitored socket region.

Review socket fit and comfort.

Consider taking a break and inspecting the residual limb.

Consult your prosthetist if discomfort persists.

This is much more useful than showing a pressure value without context.

3. Intelligent mechanical diagnostics
Combine joint-angle readings, IMU data and actuator feedback (if your design includes an actuator).

The system can:

Identify unusual joint movement.

Detect unexpected actuator behaviour.

Compare mechanical response with expected behaviour.

Flag potential maintenance requirements.

This is particularly useful for your modular design because the system could help identify which component needs inspection.

4. Adaptive movement assistance
This is the feature that could make your prosthesis significantly more advanced.

The sensors identify what the user is doing, and a safety-verified controller can adjust a variable-resistance mechanism accordingly.

